BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a5a29203f4c71d89@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260814T191151Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Continuing the Fight Against the Opioid Epide
 mic
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nOver the five years leading up to 2019\, more than 1\,600 people in
  Maine died from drug overdoses. We were losing a person every day. About 
 8% of all babies in Maine were born substance-exposed—that's three each 
 day. The life-saving drug Naloxone was not generally available. There were
  few treatment beds\, and when someone was ready to get into recovery\, ev
 en fewer treatment options were available if they didn't have health insur
 ance.\nWhen I took office\, I knew that what Maine had been doing for so m
 any years just wasn't working. We needed a coordinated response that broke
  down the silos of government\, set aside the stigma\, and spoke out about
  every aspect of the opioid epidemic. I promised that we would put the ful
 l force of my Administration behind the families who lost loved ones\, the
  businesses who lost employees\, and all the communities that have been di
 minished by this public health crisis.\nSo my first executive order\, sign
 ed on my first day in office\, fulfilled the will of the people of Maine a
 nd expanded MaineCare to more than 90\,000 people. Today\, nearly 40\,000 
 people receive treatment for substance use disorder through MaineCare. Exp
 anding health insurance was a critical first step to confronting the opioi
 d crisis.\nI named Gordon Smith as my Director of Opioid Response\, and I 
 signed the second executive order with basic strategies and specific actio
 n steps. That order established a Prevention and Recovery Cabinet made up 
 of commissioners from 14 government agencies and the Attorney General's Of
 fice and the Judicial Branch.\nWe outlined key steps to preventing overdos
 es and deaths\, including making Naloxone widely available statewide\, and
  since early 2019\, we've distributed more than 870\,000 doses of Naloxone
 \, which has saved thousands of lives.\nWe then outlined steps to expand e
 ffective treatment and recovery measures\, including training 250 recovery
  coaches and funding recovery coaches in ten \nhttp://www.maine.gov/govern
 or/mills/news/radio_address/continuing-fight-against-opioid-epidemic-2026-
 08-14
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/continuing-figh
 t-against-opioid-epidemic-2026-08-14
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
