UID:fede8c02af10f6df@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111846Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 7/24/2026: Reminders in Preparation for Maine
  eCourts and eFiling in Hancock and Washington County Courts
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nReminders in Preparation for Maine eCourts and 
 eFiling in Hancock and Washington County Courts\nDate:\n7/24/2026\nOn Mond
 ay\, July 27\, 2026\, the Maine Judicial Branch is expanding Maine eCourts
  to all case types in Judicial Region 7\, comprising Hancock and Washingto
 n County Unified Criminal Dockets (UCDs)\, Hancock and Washington County S
 uperior Courts\, and Ellsworth\, Calais\, and Machias District Courts.\nAl
 l case types -- civil\, family\, juvenile and criminal cases -- will be in
 cluded in the transition from paper filing to electronic filing. As of Mon
 day\, July 27\, all mandatory electronic filers pursuant to the Maine Rule
 s of Electronic Court Systems (MRECS) will be required to eFile documents 
 in these courts. Self-represented litigants (parties who do not have attor
 neys) who file fewer than six cases in a calendar year will not be require
 d to eFile.\nCourt Closures and Emergency Filing Procedures\nTo prepare fo
 r the implementation of Maine eCourts and eFiling on July 27\, Region 7 co
 urts are operating on a modified schedule and have emergency filing proced
 ures in place. As a reminder\, Monday\, July 27th through Friday\, July 31
 st is an Administrative Week\, and all courts will be closed from 8 a.m. t
 o noon. Please refer to the schedule of closures and emergency procedures 
 during these closings:\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/ecourts/maine-ecourts
 -region7-notice-closures.pdf\nShareFile Discontinued in Region 7 Courts\nT
 he ShareFile platform used in Region 7 courts are now disabled for all cri
 minal\, civil violation\, and juvenile filings\, as well as GAL vouchers\,
  and attorney billing procedures\, as of 2:00 p.m. on Thursday\, July 23. 
 Starting Monday\, July 27\, attorneys must instead use eFileMaine.\nTraini
 ng Resources\nFor attorneys and staff who are new to using eFileMaine\, \n
 https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13356327
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13356327
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:46da49013e6e2bb9@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111846Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 7/13/2026: Aroostook County Adult Treatment &
  Recovery Court Accepts First Participant
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nAroostook County Adult Treatment & Recovery Cou
 rt Accepts First Participant\nDate:\n7/13/2026\nThe Maine Judicial Branch 
 marked a significant milestone in expanding access to evidence-based treat
 ment and accountability by admitting the first participant to the Aroostoo
 k County Adult Treatment & Recovery (TRC) Court on July 10. The TRC's inau
 gural session at the Aroostook County Superior Courthouse in Caribou\, is 
 the newest addition to Maine's statewide system of Treatment & Recovery Co
 urts and extends access to evidence-based treatment court services through
 out northern Maine. TRCs help court-involved individuals address the under
 lying causes of criminal behavior while promoting accountability\, recover
 y\, and public safety.\nJudicial Branch Superior Court Justice Stephen Nel
 son presided over the court session\, which followed the TRC team's first 
 multidisciplinary staffing session earlier that morning. The admission rep
 resents the culmination of more than two years of legislative action\, sta
 tewide planning\, interagency collaboration\, and training to establish th
 e court. "This is an exciting day for Aroostook County\," said Justice Nel
 son. "I am delighted that we have an interagency team trained and excited 
 to implement this program and that we have accepted our first participant.
  The TRC is an important resource for the Aroostook community."\nSupreme J
 udicial Court Chief Justice Valerie Stanfill noted that the TRCs opening r
 eflects years of collaborative effort. "The Aroostook TRCs opening is an i
 mportant milestone for the Judicial Branch and reflects years of collabora
 tion with the Legislature\, the Maine Department of Health and Human Servi
 ces\, the Governor's Office\, the Office of the Attorney General\, the Aro
 ostook County District Attorney\, the Maine Commissio\nhttps://www.courts.
 maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13356241
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13356241
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8fa2e09dbf1308de@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260802T111844Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Harbor View Corporation v. Robert V. Degennar
 o and Tan\, Turtle Tavern Too
DESCRIPTION:1 \nSTATE OF MAINE     BUSINESS & CONSUMER DOCKET \nCUMBERLAND
 \, ss     CIVIL ACTION  \n       DOCKET NO. BCD-CV-18-35 \n    \n       \n
 HARBOR VIEW CORPORATION \nd/b/a KIMBALL TERRACE INN\, \n    \n      \n  Pl
 aintiff      ORDER \n         \nv.         \n    \n \nROBERT V. DEGENNARO 
 and TAN\,    \nTURTLE TAVERN TOO \n         \n \n  Defendants & \n  Third-
 Party Plaintiffs \n \nv. \n \nSTEVEN FOSS\, MARGIE M. MORSE-FOSS \nand MOF
 O\, LLC\, \n \n  Third-Party Defendants \n \nBefore the court are cross -m
 otions for summary judgment filed by plaintiff Harbor View \nCorporation (
 “Harbor View”) and defendants and third-party plaintiffs Robert DeGenn
 aro and Tan \nTurtle Tavern Too (“DeGennaro”).  The plaintiff is repre
 sented by  Attorney Christopher Largay. \nThe def endant and third -party 
 plaintiffs are represented by Attorneys Caleb Gannon and Jack \nBaldacci. 
 Third-party defendants Steven Foss\, Maggie Morse -Foss and Mofo\, LLC (th
 e “Foss \ndefendants”) are represented by  Attorney Richard Silver.  F
 or the following reasons\, the court \nconcludes that (1) it does not have
  jurisdiction over plaintiff’s complaint and (2) the third -party \ncomp
 laint should be stayed. \n \n 2 \nBackground \n This case arises out of a 
 commercial lease dispute. On November 14\, 2015\, Har bor View \nand DeGen
 naro entered into a 5 -year commercial lease agreement. (Pl.’s Supp’g 
 S.M.F. ¶  1\; \nDeGennaro Supp’g S.M.F. ¶ 2.) After entering into the 
 lease\, DeGennaro operated a restaurant at \nthe premises which was connec
 ted to a hotel owned by Harbor V iew. (Pl.’s Opp’g S.M.F. ¶  3\; \nDe
 Gennaro Supp’g S.M.F. ¶¶ 1 -3\, 5.) On August 29\, 2016\, DeGennaro an
 d the Foss defendants \nexecuted a document known as the Management and De
 ferred Purchase Agreement. (Pl.’s Supp’g \nS.M.F. ¶ 2\; Def.’s Supp
 ’g S.M.F. ¶ 13.) \n On M arch 5\, 2018\, Harbor View filed a complaint 
 against DeGennaro alleging that \nDeGennaro had breached the lease agreeme
 nt. In its complaint\, Harbor View sought a declaration \npursuant to 14 M
 .R.S. § 5953 that DeGennaro had violated the Lease \nhttps://www.courts.m
 aine.gov/courts/bcd/cv-orders/CV-18-35/BCD-CV-18-35-Harborview-v-DeGennaro
 .pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/bcd/cv-orders/CV-18-35/BCD-CV-18-3
 5-Harborview-v-DeGennaro.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7844e5bc9f3d9c47@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260802T083607Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Page updated: Maine Board of Bar Examiners 
 — Announcements
DESCRIPTION:This page's content changed since the last check. Visit the pa
 ge directly to review the update.\n\nWhat changed (text excerpt):\nAdded:\
 n  + Announcements\n  + Posted: March 11\, 2025\n  + FEE INCREASE FOR ALL 
 APPLICATIONS BEGINNING MARCH 15\, 2025\n  + The Maine Supreme Judicial Cou
 rt has increased the fees for all applications made to the Maine Board of 
 Bar Examiners. The amendment to the Maine Bar Admission Rules can be found
  at the following link:\n  + 2025_mr_01_bar_admission_2025-03-07.pdf\n  + 
 This increase will take effect for all applications received for the July 
 2025 Maine Bar Exam\, and all applications for Admission by Motion and UBE
  Score Transfer that arrive in the office beginning on March 15\, 2025. Th
 e new fees that will begin on March 15\, 2025 are as follows:\n  + Maine B
 ar Exam Application Fees:\n  + Applicants Never Admitted in Any Jurisdicti
 on or Admitted in Another Jurisdiction for Less than One Year – $800\n  
 + Applicants Admitted in Another Jurisdiction for One Year or More: $900 (
 plus NCBE Character and Fitness Application fee paid directly to NCBE)\n  
 + Admission by Motion Application Fee:\n  + $1000 (plus NCBE Character and
  Fitness Application fee paid directly to NCBE)\n  + UBE Score\n  + Transf
 er Application Fees:\n  + Applicants Seeking Admission Pursuant to Rule 11
 B Not Admitted in Another Jurisdiction for a Year or More: $900\n  + Appli
 cants Seeking Admission Pursuant to Rule 11B Admitted in Another Jurisdict
 ion for a Year or More: $1000 (plus the NCBE Character and Fitness Applica
